# cli/build.ts pins a SHA-256 over the raw bytes of every file in
# cli/licenses/ (AUDITED_LICENSE_TREE_SHA256). Git's Windows checkout would
# otherwise rewrite their LF endings to CRLF, changing those bytes and failing
# `cli:build` with "CLI license texts changed; refresh SBOM.md and the license
# audit before release" on windows runners only. Marking them non-text keeps
# the working tree byte-identical on every platform.
cli/licenses/** -text
# Upstream license texts that scripts/pdfium/rust-notices.ts copies into those
# notices verbatim: a CRLF checkout made the rendered notices differ on
# windows runners only ("Rust dependency notices are stale", cli-verify run
# 36192226215, first difference "MIT License\r").
scripts/pdfium/*-LICENSE.txt -text

# Same class of problem, different blast radius: the installers.
#
# A Windows checkout hands these back as CRLF, and two things break at once.
# The hosted-installer freshness test compares an assembly joined with "\n"
# against a file read as "\r\n" — that is the win32-x64 and win32-arm64
# failure in cli-release run 34751204216. Far worse, a CRLF site/public/cli.sh
# served to `curl | sh` begins `#!/bin/sh\r`, which is a bad interpreter on
# every Unix that matters.
*.sh text eol=lf
*.ps1 text eol=lf
# Git hooks run under sh on every platform, Git for Windows included.
.githooks/* text eol=lf
